 4.1 In ordinary mode [F6] bounds the base description length, operation count, and width polynomially. In oracle mode the same bounds follow directly from the explicit code: a machine started on $n$ input cells and run for $T(n)$ transitions can print at most $n+T(n)+1$ bits, each record occupies at least one bit, and the width is unary. The clocks and bit lengths of $a,b,\delta,s$ are polynomial, so $r$ is polynomial. In either mode the repeated circuit uses exactly $r g(C_n)+n(r-1)$ operations and $r w(C_n)$ wires. Copying a record changes each wire index by a block offset with $O(\log(rw(C_n)+2))$ bits; the unary total-wire header has length $r w(C_n)$, and the extra CNOT list has $n(r-1)$ records. In oracle mode each original record's explicit list of wires is copied in full, its port/variant identifier is unchanged, and the entire descriptor table is copied once. Hence the complete repeated description has polynomial length and is produced in polynomial time by running the supplied machines, scanning and copying the base list, and adding offsets by binary arithmetic. These are polynomially many operations on polynomially many bits, implementable by repeated scans on the one-tape machine. A serial layering gives depth at most the total operation count. [F1, F5, F6, F7, algebra]
 
